{"message":"Ok, but you keep leaning on “civilization-scale” or “existential” risk. Let’s remove that.

Say the best evidence says the growth-first change would not threaten human survival or global civilization. It would mean a warmer, more degraded planet: major biodiversity loss, damaged oceans/forests, more regional disasters, and a lot of nonhuman life permanently lost. But the human development upside is huge: many countries reach middle-income status decades earlier, child mortality falls sharply, and the poorest people get reliable energy, roads, clinics, food storage, etc.

In that version, do nonhuman ecosystems and species still get enough weight to make you reject the change? Or do you accept it because the worst outcomes are “only” ecological and regional rather than civilizational?"}

