attachments.b7baa02dc53bc1b1148ed54b1ad8961a:
{"message":"Your rule sounds almost absolute: permanent ecological loss is a redline.

Let me stress-test that with a deliberately small case. A road/clinic/electrification project would very likely save or materially improve millions of human lives over the next 20 years. The only irreversible ecological cost is extinction of one narrowly endemic insect species and permanent loss of a small patch of habitat. No cascade, no climate relevance, no known ecosystem service, no human cultural attachment. Independent review says rerouting makes the project infeasible for at least 25 years.

Do you still reject? If yes, are you really saying one obscure species can outweigh millions of human life-years? If no, then your redline isn’t irreversibility itself — so what is it?"}
